---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { site } from '../../site';

// The sample is shown from its own tested source, read at build time.
import sourceAddon from '../../../../../examples/source-addon/addon.mjs?raw';
import sourceServer from '../../../../../examples/source-addon/server.mjs?raw';

const tree = (path: string) => `${site.repoUrl}/tree/main/${path}`;

const indexExample = `
import { createWorkIndex, idsFromTags, lookupWork, splitAuthors } from '@lelap/sdk';

const index = createWorkIndex(
  files.map((file) => ({
    item: file,
    title: file.tags.album,
    authors: splitAuthors(file.tags.artist),
    ids: idsFromTags(file.tags), // ISBN, ASIN, AUDIBLE_ASIN, ----:com.apple.iTunes:ASIN …
  })),
);

// In your stream handler:
const work = await lookupWork(id); // isbn:/asin: as they are; ol: from Open Library
const matches = work ? index.find(work) : []; // [{ item, strength: 'id' | 'title' }]
`;

const run = `
cd examples/source-addon
pnpm test            # Vitest, with Open Library answered by the test
`;

const fromNpm = `
npm pkg delete dependencies.@lelap/sdk devDependencies.vitest
npm install @lelap/sdk
node server.mjs      # http://localhost:8787/manifest.json
`;
---

<Docs
  title="Mapping your files to works"
  description={`How a source add-on, and ${site.name} itself, finds which of your files a book page is about.`}
  toc={[
    ['ids', 'What you are asked for'],
    ['rules', 'Matching rules'],
    ['sdk', 'The matcher in the SDK'],
    ['sample', 'A sample source add-on'],
    ['app', 'Your own storage in the app'],
  ]}
>
  <h1>Mapping your files to works</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A book’s page in {site.name} shows a work from a catalog add-on, such as an Open Library work.
    If your add-on has audio for it, it has to know which of its files that work is. The SDK carries
    the same matcher {site.name} uses for the listener’s own library.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ids">What you are asked for</h2>
  <p>
    {site.name} asks every add-on with a <code>stream</code> resource once per page, with the first
    of the work’s IDs the add-on declares: the work’s own ID, then its <code>externalIds</code> as{' '}
    <code>prefix:value</code>. A work from the Open Library catalog add-on is{' '}
    <code>ol:OL893415W</code>, often with <code>asin:…</code>; Hardcover adds <code>isbn:…</code>.
    Declare the prefixes you can answer on the resource:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`resources: [{ name: 'stream', types: ['audiobook'], idPrefixes: ['ol:', 'isbn:', 'asin:'] }]`}
    label="manifest"
  />
  <p>
    An add-on that declares <code>ol:</code> is asked with the Open Library ID even when the work
    also has an ASIN, so it must find its files from the work itself, not only from IDs it already
    knows. No files is an empty <code>streams</code> list, never an error.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rules">Matching rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>IDs first.</strong> The same ISBN (ISBN-10 and ISBN-13 are the same number) or ASIN on
      both sides is the work. These are safe to use without asking anyone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Then title and author.</strong> Both are compared after folding case, accents and
      punctuation, dropping a leading article, notes such as “(Unabridged)” and a subtitle, and
      reading “Herbert, Frank” as Frank Herbert. Both sides need an author: a title alone matches
      nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>People, not words.</strong> Authors match on the surname, and on the first given
      name’s initial when both have one, so Stephen King is not Owen King.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Series numbers.</strong> “Harry Potter, Book 2”, “(Dune, #1)” and a folder named “03 -
      The Dragon Reborn” carry a number that is set apart. Two different numbers are two books.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Editions.</strong> A file with its own ISBN where the work has another is likely a
      different edition; it still matches by title, after the others.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A title match is a good guess, not a fact: {site.name} asks the listener before it uses one.
    Your add-on’s answers are listed as sources the listener picks from, so a title match there is
    fine; keep your tags right and IDs do the work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sdk">The matcher in the SDK</h2>
  <p>
    All of it is in <code>@lelap/sdk</code> (MIT). Everything but <code>lookupWork</code> is pure:
    no requests, no state. It needs <code>@lelap/sdk</code> 0.4.0 or later.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Function</th>
          <th scope="col">What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>createWorkIndex(entries)</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            An index of your items by ID and title. <code>find(work)</code> returns the items that
            share an ID, else those with the same title and author; <code>byId(id)</code> looks up
            one ID.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>matchStrength(work, book)</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            <code>'id'</code>, <code>'title'</code> or <code>null</code> for one pair.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>idsFromTags(tags)</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            ISBNs and ASINs from tags by name in any spelling, checked and normalised (ISBNs as 13
            digits).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>lookupWork(id, options)</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            What a work ID stands for: <code>isbn:</code> and <code>asin:</code> as they are, an{' '}
            <code>ol:</code> work’s title, authors and edition IDs from Open Library. Runs on your
            server.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>titleKey</code>, <code>authorKey</code>, <code>sameAuthor</code>,{' '}
            <code>splitAuthors</code>, <code>normalizeId</code>
          </td>
          <td>The pieces, for an index of your own.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock code={indexExample} label="Your add-on" />

  <h2 id="sample">A sample source add-on</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={tree('examples/source-addon')}>
      <code>examples/source-addon</code>
    </a>{' '}
    is plain Node with the SDK: a file list with tags, an index built from it, and a{' '}
    <code>stream</code> handler. It answers the Open Library catalog add-on’s pages for its two
    books, one by the ISBN in its tags and one by title and author, with no code beyond the file
    list.
  </p>
  <details>
    <summary>
      <code>examples/source-addon/addon.mjs</code>
    </summary>
    <CodeBlock code={sourceAddon} />
  </details>
  <CodeBlock code={sourceServer} label="examples/source-addon/server.mjs" />
  <CodeBlock code={run} label="Terminal" />
  <p>
    In the repository the sample uses the SDK’s TypeScript source, which only its tests can load.
    Copy the folder out to run it with Node and SDK 0.4.0 or later from npm:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={fromNpm} label="Terminal" />

  <h2 id="app">Your own storage in the app</h2>
  <p>
    For a folder on the phone and the servers added in the app, such as WebDAV, S3, SMB, Jellyfin or
    Plex, {site.name} does this mapping itself, with the same rules, on the device. No request
    leaves the phone for it, so no add-on and no server learns what is in the library.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      ISBNs and ASINs come from the files’ tags (ID3 <code>TXXX</code> and MP4 freeform items),
      Audiobookshelf’s book details and Jellyfin’s provider IDs. A shared one links the book on its
      own.
    </li>
    <li>
      The same title and author shows <strong>In your library?</strong> on the book’s page, for one
      tap. Folder and file names count only for files without tags.
    </li>
    <li>
      A confirmed link is remembered and syncs with the book’s location, such as{' '}
      <code>webdav:host/path</code>. The listener’s copy is listed first and plays; place, Up next
      and stats are the library book’s.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Match with catalog…</strong> on a library book searches the catalogs by hand and takes
      the work’s title, author and cover. Unlinking is one tap, on either side.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
